{
  "question": "Got reject 22 on a Victoza claim, what now?",
  "status": "answered",
  "assumption": "Assuming NY Medicaid (NYRx) and that \"reject 22\" is the NCPDP code for missing/invalid DAW code.",
  "answer": "Assuming NY Medicaid: NCPDP reject 22 = missing/invalid DAW code. Victoza® is a BLTG (Brand Less Than Generic) preferred GLP-1 on the PDL, and the generic liraglutide is non-preferred.\n\n1. Resubmit with **DAW 9** (substitution allowed by prescriber, plan requests brand). Don't send DAW 1.\n2. Use DAW 1 only if the prescriber wrote \"Brand Medically Necessary\" on the Rx and DAW 1 is on the claim.\n3. If it then rejects for PA, note the clinical criteria: PA is required if the patient is also on a DPP-4 inhibitor. The prescriber must request the PA. If you can't reach the prescriber, you can get a 72-hour emergency supply PA.\n\nThe PDL I read is revised 9/14/2026. It notes some preferred-status changes effective 10/22/2026, but I didn't see one specific to Victoza.",
